Progress bars agents show in the chat, and their logs, server side.

Agents in this deployment get a sys_session_progress tool (the runner's
0014-agent-progress.patch). The runner posts each call to
POST /v1/sessions/{id}/progress, and the lines of a log file the agent names
to POST /v1/sessions/{id}/progress/{bar}/log (omnigent_progress_api, mounted
here). omnigent_progress (images/server/omnigent_progress.py) keeps each
session's last bars and their logs in memory and publishes the session's
bars as a session.progress event on every change; the snapshot carries them
as `progress`. The web UI reads a log from GET .../progress/{bar}/log.

A bar belongs to a turn. _publish_status reports every session status to
omnigent_progress.on_status, which ends the newest bar when the session stops
working; one that works again within 5 seconds, as Claude's terminal does
between steps, carries on with it. A message from the user, seen where the
events route accepts it, makes the agent's next update start a new bar.

